# BenOS-rs

A small bare-metal ARM kernel written in Rust (`no_std` / `no_main`) and built
with Bazel. It boots on QEMU today, prints over the PL011 UART, and echoes back
whatever you type.

Its point is the build: a board is described in exactly one place
(`//boards:boards.bzl`) and everything downstream — the target platform, the
crate_universe config settings, the arch-specific boot code, the kernel ELF and
the QEMU launcher — is generated from that table.

## Supported boards

| Board         | Triple                 | Arch boot code | QEMU machine                          |
| ------------- | ---------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| `versatilepb` | `armv7a-none-eabi`     | `//arch/arm`   | `qemu-system-arm -M versatilepb`      |
| `raspi4b`     | `aarch64-unknown-none` | `//arch/arm64` | `qemu-system-aarch64 -M raspi4b`      |

A board entry without a `qemu = ...` field is build-only: it still produces an
ELF, it just gets no launcher.

## Requirements

- [Bazel](https://bazel.build) (bzlmod; see `MODULE.bazel`) — Bazelisk works fine
- QEMU (`qemu-system-arm`, `qemu-system-aarch64`) for running the images
- Nothing else: the Rust 1.97.1 toolchains, the bare-metal targets, Python and
  the crates are all fetched by Bazel

The `exec_triple` in `MODULE.bazel` is currently pinned to
`aarch64-apple-darwin`, so building on another host means adding the matching
`rust.repository_set` blocks.

## Build and run

```sh
# Cross-compile one board's kernel ELF
bazel build //kernel:versatilepb

# Every board at once
bazel build //kernel/...

# Boot it in QEMU (serial is wired to stdio; Ctrl-A X to quit)
bazel run //kernel:versatilepb.run
bazel run //kernel:raspi4b.run
```

Extra QEMU flags can be appended after `--`:

```sh
bazel run //kernel:raspi4b.run -- -d int
```

Two environment variables are honoured by the launcher
(`//tools:qemu_runner.sh`):

- `QEMU_DISPLAY` — overrides the board's `-display` value (default `none`)
- `QEMU_GDB=1` — adds `-s -S`, so QEMU waits for a debugger on `tcp::1234`

```sh
QEMU_GDB=1 bazel run //kernel:versatilepb.run
# in another shell
gdb-multiarch bazel-bin/kernel/versatilepb -ex 'target remote :1234'
```

## Layout

```
arch/arm, arch/arm64   Pre-kernel_main boot code (head.S) per triple.
                       Both export crate name `arch`, so kernel/boot.rs
                       uses the same `use arch as _;` for every board.
boards/                boards.bzl — the board/triple tables — plus the
                       per-board linker scripts.
kernel/                boot.rs (kernel_main) and the PL011 UART driver.
platforms/             platform() targets, one per triple.
platforms/settings/    config_setting() targets crate_universe selects on.
tools/                 kernel_image macro and the QEMU runner script.
Kconfig                Kconfig root, wired up via rules_kconfig (skeleton).
```

Board identity does not travel through Bazel constraints. `kernel_image` bakes
it in as `--cfg target_board="<board>"`, and passes a `--check-cfg` listing every
legal value, so a typo in `#[cfg(target_board = "...")]` is a compile error
rather than a silently dropped branch. That is how `kernel/uart.rs` picks its
PL011 base address.

## Adding a board

1. Add a linker script under `boards/`.
2. Add an entry to `BOARDS` in [boards/boards.bzl](boards/boards.bzl), naming
   its triple, linker script, and optionally its QEMU config.
3. If the triple is new, add it to `TRIPLES` in the same file, add an arch
   package supplying `head.S`, and mirror the triple into the
   `rust.repository_set` / `supported_platform_triples` lists in
   [MODULE.bazel](MODULE.bazel). (MODULE.bazel is restricted Starlark and
   cannot `load()` boards.bzl, so that one duplication is unavoidable.)

`//kernel:<board>`, `//kernel:<board>.run`, `//platforms:<triple>` and
`//platforms/settings:<triple>` all appear automatically.

## Status

Early. What works: boot, bss zeroing, stack setup, `kernel_main`, PL011 UART
in/out. There is no interrupt handling, MMU setup, scheduler or allocator yet,
and the `versatilepb` linker script still uses a Raspberry Pi-style
`KERNEL_BASE` (see the FIXME in [boards/versatilepb.ld](boards/versatilepb.ld)).

